<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav Struve</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gustav von Struve</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav Struve was a German lawyer, journalist and radical republican who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hecker">Friedrich Hecker</a>, led the revolutionary risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1848. After the failure of his own attempt in September 1848 he was imprisoned, freed during the Baden revolution of 1849, and then fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he fought for the Union in the Civil War before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-struve.jpg" alt="Gustav Struve" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustav_struve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Struve was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, the son of Johann Christoph Gustav von Struve, a Russian diplomat of the lesser nobility who served at embassies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, Munich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and later as ambassador at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>. He studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, worked in the civil service of Oldenburg from 1829 to 1831 and in 1836 settled as a lawyer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>. As editor of the Mannheimer Journal he defended Baden's liberal deputies, was repeatedly sentenced to prison and was forced to give up the paper in 1846. He married Amalie Düsar in 1845 and dropped the aristocratic von in 1847.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Pre-Parliament rejected his demand for a federal republic of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Struve and Hecker organised a revolutionary assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a> on 14 April 1848. Few joined their march, which troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> halted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, and both men fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. There Struve published Die Grundrechte des deutschen Volkes and, with Karl Heinzen, drew up a plan for the republicanisation of Germany. His rising at Lörrach on 21 September 1848 failed. Freed in the May Uprising of 1849, he helped overthrow the hesitant Lorenz Brentano before Prussian troops defeated the revolutionaries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> on 23 July.</p><p>In exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1851, Struve lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, edited German papers and wrote Weltgeschichte, a universal history from a radical republican standpoint and the main work of his career. He supported John Frémont in 1856 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> in 1860, and served briefly as a Union captain under Blenker, resigning rather than serve under Prince Felix Salm-Salm. An abolitionist, he opposed plans to settle freed slaves in Liberia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A general amnesty of 1863 allowed Struve to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Lincoln named him US consul at Sonneberg in 1865, but the Thuringian states refused him recognition because of his radical writings. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1869 and died there on 21 August 1870. A vegetarian since 1832 under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>'s Émile, he wrote the first German vegetarian novel, Mandaras Wanderungen, in 1833 and founded the Stuttgart Vegetarian Society in 1868. The historian Corinna Treitel notes that he linked vegetarianism to republican self-government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustav Struve” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Germany Friedrich Hecker and <b>Gustav Struve</b> led a radical republican uprising in Baden in April 1848, Archduke John of Austria served as Imperial Regent, and Robert Blum was executed by Austrian troops.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Baden revolutionaries of 1848, led by Friedrich Hecker and <b>Gustav Struve</b>, were defeated, and in 1849 the army mutinied and the grand duke fled until Prussian troops restored him.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustav Struve"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
